s struggling to find motivation  celebrities they're just like us  actor Bill Murray partnered up with his  local gym to promote their at home  workouts a service many studios started  to offer Kali reikus is a former TV  producer who took a leap of faith two  years ago and opened a 7,000 square foot  fitness facility in Toronto before the  lockdown the studio offered 16 classes  serving several hundred members on a  daily basis the pandemic required  pivoting renting out weights and leading  workouts online it was about finding  connection in a physically distant world  of course it's different to teach people  online than it is teach them in person  however I think we need to take a minute  to reflect on how privileged and lucky  we are to be in a time where  we can still connect with people face to  face and of course another really  amazing benefit of the virtual world  that we've kind of discovered is that we  can serve more people from you know more  places farther away and while there's no  shortage of motivation online finding  fitness gear continues to be an exercise  in patience and endurance last March in  the US retail sales for training  equipment surged by 130 percent compared  to last year  that demand simply exceeding the supply  the last month or two the supplies have  been very low because our suppliers have  run out of inventory they're very close  to catching up and a lot of our  shipments are coming in now so we'll be  able to fulfill the hundreds of orders  that we've been getting turns out the  supply chain for fitness equipment  especially free weights had the same  vulnerabilities as personal protective  equipment most of it made overseas  making them hard to get during a  worldwide pandemic but necessity is the  mother of invention and so many  Canadians have adapted finding unique  ways and objects to work out with select  gyms reopens across Canada in early June  like Club 16 in BC with new protocols  and reduced capacity meanwhile gyms in  Toronto remain closed but an upcoming  yoga events is still pushing ahead  allowing participants to step out of  their at-home bubble into another one  but the fitness industry may never be  the same we know one of the main  barriers to people exercising is the  fear of going into a gym in the first  place you know what where do you look  where do you go where that what kind of  equipment do you use even those who  didn't experience that anxiety going  into a gym may now experience it for  some individuals will be very  anxiety-provoking  addressing physical and mental health is  something that gyms will have to tackle  once doors reopen in some cases doors  that never opened in the first place  tried space yoga was set to open in the  spring but kovin 19 put a pause on the  poses owner Jordanna Ricketts  acknowledges the lockdown could bankrupt  her but says Canadians will eventually  mean more than just a physical reset  they'll need a mental one after months  of isolation when you appeal to their  sites the sounds with the music and the  smells with the incense  the sage that we have burning at all  times here all of those factors combined  is really what's gonna draw people here  and really get them to feel that feeling  of relaxation leasable essary finds that  calm by working out it's not just a  physical necessity it's also a way for  her to stay connected to her late father  these are a special way they're my dad's  weights so they've been in my basement  as a child you know he had a lot of  physical challenges in his later years  right but you know what he was out there  walking she is unsure if she will ever  step back into a gym she's just happy to  continue being active blazing her own  trail  [Music]
 

